    "fibre-cutaway": {
      "path": "assets/fibre-cutaway-v1.png",
      "description": "Magnified conceptual cutaway of a straight optical fibre with an amber core, glass cladding and teal protective coating. Colours and proportions are illustrative.",
      "prompt": "+Use case: scientific-educational.\nAsset type: text-free hero illustration for a layered scientific infographic.\nPrimary request: Create a refined scientific editorial illustration of ONE optical fibre in a longitudinal cutaway. A straight horizontal cylindrical glass cladding surrounds one thinner concentric amber-tinted glass core; a matte dark-teal protective polymer coating covers the far-right portion only. The near LEFT circular cut face clearly reveals the concentric layers. Remove the front upper quadrant of the cladding over the central length to expose the core continuously. The fibre axis must remain perfectly straight and horizontal. View from slightly above and from the left so the circular end is an ellipse.\nComposition: landscape 1536 by 1024, entire object visible, occupies approximately x=12% to 88% and y=30% to 70%; generous empty space above and below. No extra objects.\nStyle: highly crafted 3D scientific plate, subtle engraved material detail, realistic translucent glass, satin polymer, fine crisp edges, restrained studio illumination, warm ivory and petrol-teal palette with a soft amber core. The materials and cutaway should be the focus.\nBackground: genuinely transparent alpha, no ground plane, no cast shadow outside the object.\nScientific constraints: core and cladding are coaxial solid glass regions with distinct refractive index, not a hollow pipe or electrical wire. This is a magnified conceptual view, not a fabrication drawing.\nNo light beams, rays or waveforms: these will be added separately as exact vectors.\nNo text, letters, numbers, formulas, labels, arrows, callouts, logos, borders or watermark anywhere.",
      "provenance": "Built-in image-generation tool, 2026-09-10. No source image supplied. Model identifier and cost were not exposed. Original RGBA output, not edited in code."
    }
